1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,038
Ice melts in a glass.

2
00:00:02,038 --> 00:00:04,281
A matchstick burns down to black.

3
00:00:04,281 --> 00:00:06,623
Milk left out overnight turns sour.

4
00:00:07,023 --> 00:00:07,998
Three changes.

5
00:00:07,998 --> 00:00:10,204
In one, the stuff stays the same.

6
00:00:10,204 --> 00:00:12,784
In the other two, something new is made.

7
00:00:13,234 --> 00:00:14,658
Diya is making tea.

8
00:00:14,658 --> 00:00:19,451
The water on the stove starts to boil,
and the gas flame burns below it.

9
00:00:19,801 --> 00:00:20,903
Both need heat.

10
00:00:20,903 --> 00:00:23,256
But is the boiling water still water?

11
00:00:23,256 --> 00:00:25,039
And where does the gas go?

12
00:00:25,539 --> 00:00:29,579
By the end of this video, you will tell
a physical property from a chemical

13
00:00:29,579 --> 00:00:30,665
one at a glance.

14
00:00:31,315 --> 00:00:34,880
Every substance has properties,
features that help us know it.

15
00:00:35,230 --> 00:00:36,853
They come in two groups.

16
00:00:36,853 --> 00:00:39,542
Physical properties,
and chemical properties.

17
00:00:39,892 --> 00:00:44,587
A physical property can be measured or
seen without changing what the substance

18
00:00:44,587 --> 00:00:45,550
is made of.

19
00:00:45,950 --> 00:00:50,507
Colour, odour, which means smell,
melting point, boiling point and density.

20
00:00:50,907 --> 00:00:55,222
A chemical property shows up only when
the substance changes into something

21
00:00:55,222 --> 00:00:56,122
new.

22
00:00:56,109 --> 00:00:59,784
Whether it is an acid or a
base, and whether it burns.

23
00:00:59,784 --> 00:01:04,228
Burning is called combustion, so the
ability to burn is combustibility.

24
00:01:04,878 --> 00:01:06,563
So here is the one test.

25
00:01:06,563 --> 00:01:10,434
After you observe the property,
is it still the same substance?

26
00:01:10,834 --> 00:01:12,999
If yes, the property is physical.

27
00:01:12,999 --> 00:01:16,440
If a new substance has formed,
the property is chemical.

28
00:01:17,090 --> 00:01:19,564
Let's test both on our molecule bench.

29
00:01:19,564 --> 00:01:21,371
First, the boiling water.

30
00:01:21,721 --> 00:01:25,925
In liquid water, the molecules stay
close and slide around each other.

31
00:01:26,325 --> 00:01:30,484
Heat them, and they move faster and
faster, until they escape as vapour.

32
00:01:30,884 --> 00:01:32,964
But look at each molecule.

33
00:01:32,964 --> 00:01:35,562
Still two hydrogens on one oxygen.

34
00:01:35,562 --> 00:01:38,047
No bond inside a molecule broke.

35
00:01:38,447 --> 00:01:41,030
So boiling point is a physical property.

36
00:01:41,030 --> 00:01:43,777
The water is still water, now as a gas.

37
00:01:44,177 --> 00:01:45,492
Now the flame.

38
00:01:45,492 --> 00:01:47,877
Natural gas is mostly methane.

39
00:01:47,877 --> 00:01:51,216
One carbon atom holding
four hydrogen atoms.

40
00:01:51,616 --> 00:01:53,166
Methane meets oxygen.

41
00:01:53,166 --> 00:01:58,560
The old bonds break, the atoms move,
and the electrons pair up in new bonds.

42
00:01:58,960 --> 00:02:01,350
Out come carbon dioxide and water.

43
00:02:01,350 --> 00:02:02,880
Brand new substances.

44
00:02:02,880 --> 00:02:06,077
So the ability to burn
is a chemical property.

45
00:02:06,727 --> 00:02:08,231
Look at the counter.

46
00:02:08,231 --> 00:02:10,099
Every atom is still there.

47
00:02:10,099 --> 00:02:13,936
One carbon, four hydrogens
and four oxygens on each side.

48
00:02:13,936 --> 00:02:15,743
Only the partners changed.

49
00:02:16,143 --> 00:02:17,310
Pause and think.

50
00:02:17,310 --> 00:02:18,867
Ice melting into water.

51
00:02:18,867 --> 00:02:21,847
Is that a physical
change, or a chemical one?

52
00:02:25,047 --> 00:02:25,947
Physical!

53
00:02:25,851 --> 00:02:28,264
Ice and water are both H two O.

54
00:02:28,264 --> 00:02:31,196
Only the spacing of the molecules changed.

55
00:02:31,846 --> 00:02:35,336
One physical property we
can calculate is density.

56
00:02:35,686 --> 00:02:36,591
Step one.

57
00:02:36,591 --> 00:02:41,369
Density tells us how much mass is
packed into each unit of volume.

58
00:02:42,119 --> 00:02:43,148
Step two.

59
00:02:43,148 --> 00:02:46,561
So density equals mass divided by volume.

60
00:02:47,311 --> 00:02:48,359
Step three.

61
00:02:48,359 --> 00:02:51,686
Mass in grams, volume
in cubic centimetres.

62
00:02:51,686 --> 00:02:55,024
So density is in grams
per cubic centimetre.

63
00:02:55,774 --> 00:02:56,734
Step four.

64
00:02:56,734 --> 00:02:59,922
The SI unit is kilograms per cubic metre.

65
00:03:00,672 --> 00:03:01,705
Remember this.

66
00:03:01,705 --> 00:03:04,224
A physical property keeps the substance.

67
00:03:04,224 --> 00:03:06,608
A chemical property makes a new one.

68
00:03:07,308 --> 00:03:09,043
Now, one worked example.

69
00:03:09,343 --> 00:03:14,147
A metal block measures two centimetres,
by two centimetres, by two point

70
00:03:14,147 --> 00:03:15,430
five centimetres.

71
00:03:15,430 --> 00:03:17,700
Its mass is twenty seven grams.

72
00:03:17,700 --> 00:03:19,046
Find its density.

73
00:03:19,546 --> 00:03:20,720
Volume first.

74
00:03:20,720 --> 00:03:25,222
Two times two times two point
five is ten cubic centimetres.

75
00:03:25,972 --> 00:03:28,277
Density is mass over volume.

76
00:03:28,277 --> 00:03:32,212
Twenty seven grams divided
by ten cubic centimetres.

77
00:03:32,962 --> 00:03:36,109
That is two point seven
grams per cubic centimetre.

78
00:03:36,859 --> 00:03:38,253
That matches aluminium.

79
00:03:38,253 --> 00:03:41,798
And after we measure it, the
block is still the same metal.

80
00:03:42,448 --> 00:03:44,677
Let's sort a few everyday changes.

81
00:03:45,077 --> 00:03:46,801
Sugar dissolving in water.

82
00:03:46,801 --> 00:03:50,125
Boil the water away, and
the sugar is still there.

83
00:03:50,125 --> 00:03:51,025
Physical.

84
00:03:51,257 --> 00:03:53,186
Iron rusting in damp air.

85
00:03:53,186 --> 00:03:57,760
Iron reacts with oxygen and water
to form rust, a new substance.

86
00:03:57,760 --> 00:03:58,660
Chemical.

87
00:03:58,974 --> 00:04:00,715
Wax melting is physical.

88
00:04:00,715 --> 00:04:05,537
But a candle burning is chemical,
because the wax turns into carbon dioxide

89
00:04:05,537 --> 00:04:06,437
and water.

90
00:04:06,833 --> 00:04:09,052
Milk setting into curd is chemical.

91
00:04:09,052 --> 00:04:11,712
Bacteria turn the milk sugar into an acid.

92
00:04:12,362 --> 00:04:13,828
Now, the exam trap.

93
00:04:14,178 --> 00:04:17,501
Some students think any change
that needs heat is chemical.

94
00:04:17,501 --> 00:04:20,194
Boiling needs heat, so
they call it chemical.

95
00:04:20,594 --> 00:04:22,448
But heat is not the test.

96
00:04:22,448 --> 00:04:23,883
A new substance is.

97
00:04:23,883 --> 00:04:27,162
Boiling water gives water
vapour, still H two O.

98
00:04:27,162 --> 00:04:29,156
So boiling point is physical.

99
00:04:29,806 --> 00:04:31,314
Where is this asked?

100
00:04:31,314 --> 00:04:35,727
As questions that sort properties
into physical and chemical, and small

101
00:04:35,727 --> 00:04:37,064
density numericals.

102
00:04:37,664 --> 00:04:38,938
Exam level now.

103
00:04:38,938 --> 00:04:42,028
Which pair contains only
chemical properties?

104
00:04:42,028 --> 00:04:43,357
Pause and solve.

105
00:04:48,757 --> 00:04:50,197
The answer is C.

106
00:04:50,197 --> 00:04:52,240
Combustibility and basicity.

107
00:04:52,740 --> 00:04:57,230
Both show up only when the substance
reacts and turns into something new.

108
00:04:57,730 --> 00:05:00,485
A and B each hide one physical property.

109
00:05:00,485 --> 00:05:03,230
Colour and density need no new substance.

110
00:05:03,730 --> 00:05:05,477
D is the opposite case.

111
00:05:05,477 --> 00:05:08,324
Boiling point and odour are both physical.

112
00:05:08,924 --> 00:05:10,386
Here is your recap.

113
00:05:10,386 --> 00:05:11,889
Screenshot this card.

114
00:05:15,089 --> 00:05:18,087
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

115
00:05:18,487 --> 00:05:19,387
One.

116
00:05:19,019 --> 00:05:23,263
Is the melting point of ice a
physical or a chemical property?

117
00:05:24,063 --> 00:05:24,963
Two.

118
00:05:24,574 --> 00:05:27,718
Why is the ability to
burn a chemical property?

119
00:05:28,518 --> 00:05:29,418
Three.

120
00:05:29,166 --> 00:05:34,375
A stone has a mass of fifty grams and
a volume of twenty cubic centimetres.

121
00:05:34,375 --> 00:05:35,959
What is its density?

122
00:05:36,859 --> 00:05:41,577
Next time: how do scientists in every
country agree on one metre and one

123
00:05:41,577 --> 00:05:42,477
kilogram?

124
00:05:42,358 --> 00:05:46,110
We find out in measurement,
SI units and uncertainty.
